| role | name | model | serial | console | software | boot image / config |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| DUT | swi_d | AT-x960-56YSQ | A2WQS700B | /dev/u2 @115200 | sin_x96056-20261012-24 | flash:/x960-tb277.rel / flash:/default.cfg |
| partner (far end of both test links) | swi_b | AT-x960-28YSQ | A10737A261700021 | /dev/u3, NOT opened | sin_x96056-20260921-9 | flash:/backup.rel |

- **The two test links, both to swi_b and both 1000BASE-T copper SFP:**
  `swi_b port1.0.2 <-> swi_d port1.0.2` and `swi_b port1.0.16 <-> swi_d port1.0.16`.
  Confirmed independently here by `show spanning-tree brief`, which gives both ports the same
  Designated Bridge `8000:0000cd423029` -- i.e. the same far-end bridge.
- **ifIndex, measured over SNMP, not assumed:**
  `.1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.2.5002 = STRING: port1.0.2`, `...5016 = STRING: port1.0.16`,
  `...5010 = STRING: port1.0.10`. The ifIndex is 5000 + the port number on this platform, and
  5002 / 5016 are the two numbers every trap below is graded on.
- 🔴 **port1.0.10 and port1.0.48 were never shut, flapped or reconfigured.** They carry the path
  to the trap receiver; flapping one would have destroyed the instrument. The dispatch names
  port1.0.2 and port1.0.16 as the ports to use and that is what was used.
- **port1.0.2 is the RSTP root port** at baseline and port1.0.16 is Alternate/Discarding to the
  same bridge, exactly as the dispatch warned. Shutting the root port moves the root port to
  port1.0.16 and back; that is normal RSTP and it reconverged fully (STEP 7).
- Trap receiver: the box, **eth3 10.38.22.33/27**, cabled to swi_d port1.0.10 (access vlan 10,
  `interface vlan10` 10.38.22.34/27). Traps are caught with `tools/tb capture ... udp port 162`
  and decoded with `tools/snmpwire.py --port 162 --values --abs`.

🔥 **`--values` is not optional for this case.** A linkDown and a linkUp trap carry
**byte-identical varbind OIDs** -- ifIndex, ifAdminStatus and ifOperStatus, all at the same
instance. The only thing that distinguishes them is the **value** of `snmpTrapOID.0`
(`1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.5.3` vs `.5.4`). Decoding OIDs alone cannot tell the two apart at all. The
option was added to `tools/snmpwire.py` by T44308 of this group for exactly this reason.

