## STEP 2 (designed) -- baseline OFF: the GLOBAL trap group alone produces nothing

**This is the step that shows the per-port command is load-bearing.** `snmp-server enable trap
nsm` is armed globally, and neither port carries `snmp trap link-status`. A full flap of
port1.0.2 inside a 110 s capture of udp/162 (`work/s2-leg1-unarmed.log`):

    10:39:01  tcpdump -i eth3 -w s2unarmed.pcap udp port 162   (110 s)
    10:39:01 - 10:39:16  quiet
    10:39:26  shutdown port1.0.2        -> show interface: port1.0.2 ... disabled
    10:39:52  no shutdown port1.0.2     -> show interface: port1.0.2 ... connected
    10:40:52  capture closed -- 0 packets captured

    snmpwire SUMMARY 0 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 0 PLAINTEXT   (exit 1 = nothing decoded)

The flap was real: `show interface port1.0.2 status` read `disabled` between the two commands
and `connected` after. **Zero traps.** So on this platform the global group is necessary but not
sufficient, and a link trap is OFF by default on a port. **STEP 2 PASS.**

