## STEP 5 (designed) -- the control is a measurement, not a silence: the DUT SAW both flaps

A control that produces nothing is worthless if the stimulus might not have happened. The DUT's
own log settles it (`work/s8-linklog.log`, `show log | include port1.0.2|port1.0.16`, DUT clock,
add ~15 s for the box clock):

    2026 Oct 19 21:41:38 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: Port down notification received for port1.0.2
    2026 Oct 19 21:41:38 user.notice awplus MSTP[1090]: CIST root port changed: port1.0.2->port1.0.16
    2026 Oct 19 21:41:38 user.notice awplus MSTP[1090]: CIST port1.0.16 now forwarding, propagating TC to other ports
    2026 Oct 19 21:41:38 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: port1.0.2 user shutdown
    2026 Oct 19 21:42:09 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: port1.0.2 user no shutdown
    2026 Oct 19 21:42:10 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: Port up notification received for port1.0.2
    2026 Oct 19 21:42:14 user.notice awplus MSTP[1090]: CIST root port changed: port1.0.16->port1.0.2
    2026 Oct 19 21:42:14 user.notice awplus MSTP[1090]: CIST port1.0.2 now forwarding, propagating TC to other ports
    2026 Oct 19 21:42:50 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: Port down notification received for port1.0.16
    2026 Oct 19 21:42:50 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: port1.0.16 user shutdown
    2026 Oct 19 21:43:21 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: port1.0.16 user no shutdown
    2026 Oct 19 21:43:21 user.notice awplus NSM[946]: Port up notification received for port1.0.16

**The DUT detected and logged all four link transitions in exactly the same words**, two for the
armed port and two for the unarmed one -- `Port down notification received for ...` /
`Port up notification received for ...`. It emitted a trap for two of them.

⇒ **`snmp trap link-status` gates the NOTIFICATION, not the detection.** The silence for
port1.0.16 is the feature working, not the stimulus failing. That is what turns the control into
a measurement.

Correlation with the wire, applying the measured ~15 s skew (DUT behind box):
21:41:38 -> box 10:41:53 vs trap #1 at **10:41:52.459**; 21:42:10 -> box 10:42:25 vs trap #2 at
**10:42:23.920**. Both within about a second, which is the resolution of a log stamped to the
second against a capture stamped to the millisecond.

The RSTP churn is in the log too and is exactly what STEP 1 predicted: the root port moved
port1.0.2 -> port1.0.16 when the root port went down, and back when it returned.

**STEP 5 PASS.**

